.TH GIT\-TREE\-MERGED 1 "2026-09-11" "git\-tree\-merged" "Git Extras"
.SH NAME
git\-tree\-merged \- is a branch's content already in another branch (safe to delete)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git tree\-merged
.RI [ commit ]
.RB [ \-\-onto
.IR ref ]
.RB [ \-\-fetch ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.br
.B git tree\-merged \-\-self\-test
.SH DESCRIPTION
Squash, rebase and cherry\-pick all rewrite commit SHAs, so "is my work on
main?" cannot be answered by SHA or by
.BR "git branch \-\-merged" .
.PP
Comparing tree hashes answers it only while the branch is still current with
.IR ref .
The moment someone else lands first, the squash of your branch carries their
changes as well, so its tree is "your content plus theirs" and no commit on
.I ref
has \(em or can ever have \(em your tree, however cleanly your work merged.
.PP
.B git tree\-merged
asks the question that survives all of it: it merges
.I commit
into
.I ref
in memory and checks that the result changes nothing. If it does not, then
.I ref
already holds every change
.I commit
carries and the branch can be deleted without losing anything.
.PP
When a commit on
.I ref
happens to have
.IR commit 's
exact tree, it is named on a second line as a convenience. That line is a
bonus, not the verdict \(em a squash that landed on a
.I ref
which had moved on has no such commit, and the verdict stands without it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I commit
Commit to check. Defaults to
.BR HEAD .
.TP
.BI \-\-onto " ref"
Branch it should already be in. Defaults to
.BR origin/main .
.TP
.B \-\-fetch
Refresh
.I ref
from its remote first. Requires network. A
.IR remote / branch
ref is fetched from that remote; a local ref is left alone.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
No output, exit code only. Errors are still reported.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary. Note that
.B git tree\-merged \-\-help
is intercepted by
.BR git (1)
and shows this manual page instead; use
.B git tree\-merged \-h
for the short usage.
.TP
.B \-\-self\-test
Build a throwaway repository, walk a branch through squash\-merge, drift, and a
squash that lands on a target which moved on first, and assert every verdict.
Exits 0 if the checks pass.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
.I ref
already contains every change
.I commit
carries.
.TP
.B 1
It does not, or the two conflict. The files
.I commit
would still add are listed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage or setup error, such as an unknown flag or an unresolvable ref.
.SH LIMITATIONS
If
.I ref
edited the same lines
.I after
your work landed there, the in\-memory merge conflicts and the verdict is a
conservative
.BR 1 .
Your content may well be in; the tool cannot tell, so it refuses to bless a
deletion. Check that case by hand.
.PP
A stale
.I ref
gives a false
.BR 1 ,
never a false
.BR 0 .
Pass
.B \-\-fetch
when it matters.
.PP
Requires git 2.38 or newer, for
.BR "git merge\-tree \-\-write\-tree" .
.SH EXAMPLES
Is my current branch's content already on
.BR origin/main ?
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
git tree\-merged
.EE
.RE
.PP
Check a specific branch against a release branch, refreshing it first:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
git tree\-merged my\-feature \-\-onto origin/release\-1.2 \-\-fetch
.EE
.RE
.PP
Delete every local branch whose content has landed:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
git for\-each\-ref \-\-format='%(refname:short)' refs/heads |
while read \-r b; do
    git tree\-merged "$b" \-q && git branch \-D "$b"
done
.EE
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR git\-branch (1),
.BR git\-merge\-tree (1),
.BR git\-cherry (1),
.BR git\-log (1),
.BR git\-patch\-id (1)
